1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,288
Where do fractions live on the number line? Today

2
00:00:03,288 --> 00:00:06,942
we're learning how to represent rational numbers — numbers

3
00:00:06,942 --> 00:00:08,842
like p over q — as exact points on

4
00:00:08,842 --> 00:00:10,011
a number line.

5
00:00:10,367 --> 00:00:13,913
Imagine measuring a length with a rope. Here's zero,

6
00:00:13,913 --> 00:00:16,608
and here's one whole unit. But what if the

7
00:00:16,608 --> 00:00:20,224
length falls somewhere in between? We need numbers like

8
00:00:20,224 --> 00:00:24,338
one-half and three-fourths to describe exactly where it lands.

9
00:00:24,700 --> 00:00:27,821
Every rational number has an exact place on the

10
00:00:27,821 --> 00:00:31,983
number line. Here are the integers: minus two, minus

11
00:00:31,983 --> 00:00:36,065
one, zero, one, and two. Halfway between zero and

12
00:00:36,065 --> 00:00:39,827
one is one-half. Halfway between minus one and zero

13
00:00:39,827 --> 00:00:44,469
is minus one-half. And beyond one, we find three-halves.

14
00:00:44,469 --> 00:00:48,151
Fractions aren't floating symbols — they live at exact

15
00:00:48,151 --> 00:00:49,031
points.

16
00:00:49,383 --> 00:00:52,096
Let's zoom into the interval from zero to one.

17
00:00:52,096 --> 00:00:56,616
Look at this fraction: three-sevenths. The denominator, seven, tells

18
00:00:56,616 --> 00:00:58,877
us how many equal parts to divide this unit

19
00:00:58,877 --> 00:01:01,977
into. The numerator, three, tells us how many of

20
00:01:01,977 --> 00:01:05,787
those parts to count from zero. Denominator: equal parts.

21
00:01:05,787 --> 00:01:09,274
Numerator: parts counted. That's the whole secret!

22
00:01:09,633 --> 00:01:14,417
Let's locate three-sevenths, step by step. First, divide zero

23
00:01:14,417 --> 00:01:17,946
to one into seven equal parts. Now, count three

24
00:01:17,946 --> 00:01:23,122
parts from zero. One... two... three. That's exactly where

25
00:01:23,122 --> 00:01:25,004
three-sevenths lives.

26
00:01:25,367 --> 00:01:28,857
What changes for a negative rational number, like minus

27
00:01:28,857 --> 00:01:32,277
three-fourths? The rule is exactly the same — divide

28
00:01:32,277 --> 00:01:35,910
the unit into equal parts. Here, the denominator four

29
00:01:35,910 --> 00:01:39,116
means four equal parts, from minus one to zero.

30
00:01:39,116 --> 00:01:42,393
The numerator three tells us to count three parts.

31
00:01:42,393 --> 00:01:44,957
But now the negative sign means we move left

32
00:01:44,957 --> 00:01:49,018
from zero, not right. Same rule, opposite direction.

33
00:01:49,383 --> 00:01:52,228
This isn't just abstract — it's how we measure

34
00:01:52,228 --> 00:01:55,896
real things. Say a length measures three-fourths of a

35
00:01:55,896 --> 00:01:59,938
metre. Divide the one-metre ruler into four equal parts,

36
00:01:59,938 --> 00:02:03,830
and count three of them. That's exactly three-fourths of

37
00:02:03,830 --> 00:02:06,001
a metre — the same idea as our number

38
00:02:06,001 --> 00:02:06,674
line!

39
00:02:07,033 --> 00:02:10,119
Now it's your turn. Try to locate minus four-fifths

40
00:02:10,119 --> 00:02:12,745
on the number line. Pause the video and work

41
00:02:12,745 --> 00:02:14,124
it out for yourself.

42
00:02:24,933 --> 00:02:28,832
Let's check your answer. The denominator five means five

43
00:02:28,832 --> 00:02:32,131
equal parts, from minus one to zero. Now count

44
00:02:32,131 --> 00:02:35,955
four parts, moving left because of the negative sign.

45
00:02:35,955 --> 00:02:39,105
One, two, three, four — there it is: minus

46
00:02:39,105 --> 00:02:40,304
four-fifths!

47
00:02:40,667 --> 00:02:44,071
Let's lock in the method with three simple rules.

48
00:02:44,071 --> 00:02:48,156
Denominator tells you the number of equal parts. Numerator

49
00:02:48,156 --> 00:02:51,031
tells you how many parts to count. And the

50
00:02:51,031 --> 00:02:54,663
sign tells you which direction to move. Any rational

51
00:02:54,663 --> 00:02:56,932
number p over q, with q not equal to

52
00:02:56,932 --> 00:02:59,278
zero, can be located this way.

53
00:02:59,633 --> 00:03:02,902
Let's recap what we learned today. The denominator divides

54
00:03:02,902 --> 00:03:05,627
the unit into equal parts, and the numerator counts

55
00:03:05,627 --> 00:03:07,866
how many of them to take. A positive sign

56
00:03:07,866 --> 00:03:10,651
moves right, a negative sign moves left. We used

57
00:03:10,651 --> 00:03:14,828
this to locate three-sevenths, minus three-fourths, a real measurement,

58
00:03:14,828 --> 00:03:17,976
and even solved minus four-fifths together. Thank you so

59
00:03:17,976 --> 00:03:20,035
much for watching, and I will see you in

60
00:03:20,035 --> 00:03:21,124
the next lesson!
